talk(174,'1969-09-15','B. R. Vainberg','P000283','Fundamental solutions and singularities; the Bernstein problem',r'The dated September entry reports constant-coefficient PDE representations, naming H\"ormander, Borovikov and Grushin, and then the dimension threshold in the Bernstein problem. The unheaded material on P000282 is the continuation of Buchstaber\textquotesingle s earlier record 173, already indexed as 1968-173.1--3 and GP-1968-42; it is not a new Vainberg theorem. The present record ends at the separate heading \ru{Каждан и Бернштейн}.')
item(174,'P000283','construction','Superpositions on a characteristic cone',r'The notes call \(\int_{P(\xi)=0}f((x,\xi))A(\xi)\,d\xi\) the general solution of the homogeneous equation, with arbitrary \(f,A\), and attribute a singularity description to Borovikov.',r'If \(P\) is a homogeneous polynomial of degree \(m\), each smooth ridge function \(x\mapsto f(x\cdot\xi)\) with \(P(\xi)=0\) satisfies \(P(\partial_x)f(x\cdot\xi)=0\). Any superposition for which differentiation under the integral is valid is likewise a solution. Exhaustion of all local or global solutions requires a specified real or complex characteristic cycle, solution space and amplitude class.',r'Borovikov\textquotesingle s 1959 paper is the named antecedent \citep{borovikov1959fundamental}. The fragment does not identify a precise completeness or singular-support theorem from that paper. The unrestricted phrase \textquotedblleft general solution\textquotedblright{} is therefore not certified.',refs=['borovikov1959fundamental'],cls='F',literal='false as written',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction=r'The checked elementary ridge-function assertion is separated from the unverified completeness claim. For an elliptic real symbol, its real characteristic cone may reduce to the origin, so an ordinary integral over that cone cannot represent all harmonic functions.',status='As of 9 November 2026: the construction has a classical antecedent; the exact completeness and singularity claim intended in the notebook remains unidentified.',confidence='medium')
item(174,'P000283','construction','Homogeneous powers and normalized distributions',r'The displayed example has \((x,\xi)^\lambda/\Gamma(\lambda+1)\) under the characteristic-surface integral and refers to \(R_0^+\).',r'The one-variable distributions \(t_+^\lambda/\Gamma(\lambda+1)\), initially defined for \(\operatorname{Re}\lambda>-1\), have an entire distribution-valued continuation and satisfy \(\partial_t(t_+^\lambda/\Gamma(\lambda+1))=t_+^{\lambda-1}/\Gamma(\lambda)\). Applying such distributions to a linear functional is a standard ingredient of homogeneous fundamental solutions. The notebook does not specify the power branch, sign, cycle or meaning of \(R_0^+\).',r'The method belongs to the classical construction discussed by \citet{borovikov1959fundamental}; an exact formula for the unnamed operator cannot be matched from this fragment.',refs=['borovikov1959fundamental'],cls='F',match='thematic only',verification='publisher metadata checked',literal='not determinable',reconstruction=r'The positive-part convention is a modern example, not a recovered subscript or branch from the scan.',status='As of 9 November 2026: identifiable method, unidentified specific fundamental solution.',confidence='low')
item(174,'P000283','construction','Boundary values of reciprocal symbols',r'A Grushin formula is written as a sum of Fourier integrals with denominator \(P(\xi)\pm i0\) and a partition of unity on the sphere.',r'When the distributional limits \((P\pm i\varepsilon)^{-1}\to T_\pm\) exist, \(P T_\pm=1\); with the Fourier normalization fixed, \(\mathcal F^{-1}T_\pm\) is a fundamental solution of \(P(D)\). A localization partition must sum to one in the required symbol region, with the origin and any singular characteristic points treated separately.',r'Borovikov\textquotesingle s paper is a verified nearby antecedent, but targeted searches of Grushin\textquotesingle s fundamental-solution work did not identify this exact partition formula. No unrestricted existence theorem for the \(i0\) limit is inferred.',refs=['borovikov1959fundamental'],cls='F',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',status='As of 9 November 2026: the formal multiplier mechanism is clear; its intended hypotheses and precise Grushin source are not.',confidence='medium')
bern=item(174,'P000283','theorem','Dimension threshold for entire minimal graphs',r'The notes announce that an entire minimal graph \(z=f(x_1,\ldots,x_{n-1})\) is a plane for \(n\leq8\), and that the assertion fails for \(n\geq9\), naming De Giorgi, Simons and Bombieri.',r'For a smooth entire solution \(u:\mathbb R^m\to\mathbb R\) of the minimal-surface equation, every graph is affine when \(m\leq7\). Non-affine entire minimal graphs exist for every \(m\geq8\). Thus the ambient dimension threshold is \(m+1=9\).',r'The counterexamples of \citet{bombieridegiorgigiusti1969minimal} settle the remaining high-dimensional direction. This is an announcement of an already obtained result, continuing the earlier seminar occurrence 1966-114.6, rather than a new unsolved question.',refs=['bombieridegiorgigiusti1969minimal'],literal='correct',problem='GP-1966-13',verification='read the result',reconstruction='The variable dimension and the ambient dimension are explicitly distinguished; their one-unit difference explains the threshold in the notebook.')
correction('P000282','opening carryover','Catalog record 174 includes P000282','Attribute P000282 to the continuation of record 173, already indexed in the previous chapter','Source continuity and previous_index.md identify the Buchstaber torus and film/Morse discussion as 1968-173.1--3.','both','provenance')
checkpoint()

talk(175,'1969-09-15','D. A. Kazhdan','P000283-P000284','Local spectral measures and Artin approximation',r'The source heading credits Kazhdan and Bernstein jointly for the spectral-measure discussion. A second Kazhdan note reports Artin\textquotesingle s analytic approximation theorem through the Bourbaki seminar. The latter belongs to this record, before the Fichera heading on P000284.')
item(175,'P000283-P000284','definition','Pointwise spectral measure and projector kernel',r'For a self-adjoint operator \(A\), a spectral interval \(\Lambda\), and a point \(x_0\), the notes set \(M_{x_0}(\Lambda)=\max|f(x_0)|^2\) over unit vectors in the spectral subspace and introduce its projector kernel.',r'Let \(A\) be self-adjoint on \(L^2(X,\mu)\) with spectral projections \(E(\Lambda)\). If evaluation at \(x\) is a well-defined bounded functional on \(E(\Lambda)L^2\), then its squared norm is \(\sup_{\|u\|=1,u\in E(\Lambda)L^2}|u(x)|^2\). When \(E(\Lambda)\) has a sufficiently regular reproducing kernel this equals \(K_\Lambda(x,x)\). Compatible point evaluations give the usual positive local spectral measure.',r'This is the classical spectral-function construction; see \citet[definition of the spectral function]{hormander1968spectral}. Density of continuous vectors alone does not ensure bounded evaluation, existence of a maximum, or a regular projector kernel.',refs=['hormander1968spectral'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',speaker='D. A. Kazhdan and I. N. Bernstein')
item(175,'P000283-P000284','claim','Spectral convergence under domain exhaustion',r'The heading says convergence of the spectrum as the domains increase, accompanied by nested-domain sketches and the preceding local measure.',r'To formulate a convergence theorem one must specify domains \(X_j\uparrow X\), the realizations and boundary conditions of the operators, a common Hilbert-space comparison, and whether the conclusion concerns resolvents, spectral projections or local spectral measures. None of these data is recorded sufficiently to select one theorem.',r'Targeted searches for Kazhdan--Bernstein spectral-measure or domain-exhaustion results, including Russian author and topic variants, did not identify a precise publication. H\"ormander\textquotesingle s spectral-function work is thematic background, not proof of the unnamed exhaustion result.',cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',status='As of 9 November 2026: no uniquely determined claim is available for a solved/open verdict.',confidence='low',speaker='D. A. Kazhdan and I. N. Bernstein')
art1=item(175,'P000284','theorem','Analytic approximation of formal solutions',r'The Kazhdan paragraph says that an analytic system has a formal parametric solution if and only if it has a convergent one, and that convergent solutions may be chosen arbitrarily close to the formal solution.',r'For \(F\in\mathbb C\{x_1,\ldots,x_n,y_1,\ldots,y_m\}^r\) and \(\widehat y\in\mathbb C[[x]]^m\) with \(\widehat y(0)=0\) and \(F(x,\widehat y)=0\), every integer \(c\geq1\) admits \(y\in\mathbb C\{x\}^m\) satisfying \(F(x,y)=0\) and \(y-\widehat y\in(x)^c\mathbb C[[x]]^m\).',r'Published by \citet{artin1968analytic} in December 1968. The Bourbaki exposition is \citet[Thm.~1]{raynaud1969artin}, delivered by Michel Raynaud in June 1969 and printed in 1971. The notebook\textquotesingle s \textquotedblleft Artin in Bourbaki\textquotedblright{} identifies the subject, not the lecturer.',refs=['artin1968analytic','raynaud1969artin'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1969-03',reconstruction=r'\textquotedblleft Arbitrarily close\textquotedblright{} means agreement to any prescribed finite jet in the maximal-ideal topology, not convergence of the original formal series.')
correction('P000283','heading before spectral measures','Kazhdan (catalog attribution)','Kazhdan and Bernstein are jointly named in the notebook heading','The scan clearly reads Каждан и Бернштейн.; the catalogue speaker remains a record identifier, while item attribution is joint.','both','provenance')
checkpoint()

talk(176,'1969-09-22','Gaetano Fichera','P000284-P000286','The Signorini problem and unilateral constraints',r'Fichera describes elastic contact with a rigid frictionless support, its variational formulation and a scalar model. The existence problem is weak and variational; classical regularity is a separate issue. The source uses a stress sign compatible with Fichera\textquotesingle s inward-normal convention. The record ends before the dated Kazhdan entry of 29 September on P000286.')
item(176,'P000284-P000285','definition','Unilateral frictionless elastic contact',r'On \(\Sigma\), either \(u\cdot\nu=0\) with nonnegative normal reaction and zero tangential traction, or \(u\cdot\nu>0\) with zero traction. Loads are given on \(\Sigma^*=\partial A\setminus\Sigma\).',r'Let a bounded elastic body \(A\subset\mathbb R^d\) have a sufficiently regular boundary decomposed into a contact portion \(\Sigma\) and a loaded portion \(\Sigma^*\). With an explicitly chosen normal and stress convention, write the normal gap as \(g\geq0\) and compressive contact reaction as \(\lambda\geq0\). Frictionless contact is \(g\lambda=0\) and zero tangential reaction; prescribed traction acts on \(\Sigma^*\).',r'This is the Signorini problem treated in Fichera\textquotesingle s 1964 work and summarized before this lecture in \citet[pp.~64--66]{fichera1967unilateral}.',refs=['fichera1967unilateral'],reconstruction='The modern gap/reaction variables avoid silently switching the notebook\textquotesingle s inward normal and stress convention.')
item(176,'P000285','construction','Strain, constitutive law and equilibrium',r'The notebook gives \(\varepsilon_{ik}=\tfrac12(\partial_k u_i+\partial_i u_k)\), \(\sigma_{ik}+\partial W/\partial\varepsilon_{ik}=0\), and the equilibrium and traction equations.',r'For small-strain linear elasticity, the strain is the symmetric gradient \(\varepsilon(u)\). A positive-definite quadratic energy \(W(x,\varepsilon)\) defines the conventional stress \(\tau=\partial_\varepsilon W\); with outward normal \(n\), equilibrium is \(-\operatorname{div}\tau=f\) and natural boundary traction is \(\tau n=t\). Taking \(\sigma=-\tau\) and \(\nu=-n\) recovers the sign pattern in the notebook.',r'The constitutive and boundary conventions are explicit in \citet[pp.~64--65]{fichera1967unilateral}. They are pre-existing elastic theory, not a new sign-changing material law.',refs=['fichera1967unilateral'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(176,'P000285','theorem','Energy minimization and complementarity',r'The proposed energy is the elastic energy minus work of body and surface forces; a classical minimizer in the admissible class is said to solve the contact problem, and conversely.',r'Let \(V\) be a Sobolev displacement space, \(K\subset V\) its nonempty closed convex set satisfying the trace inequality, and \(J(u)=\tfrac12a(u,u)-\ell(u)\), with \(a\) continuous symmetric positive semidefinite. Then \(u\in K\) minimizes \(J\) exactly when \(a(u,v-u)\geq\ell(v-u)\) for all \(v\in K\). For sufficiently smooth \(u\), integration by parts gives the equilibrium, prescribed-traction and complementarity conditions.',r'The variational contact formulation predates the lecture; \citet[pp.~65--67]{fichera1967unilateral} gives Fichera\textquotesingle s construction. Classical differentiability is needed only to recover pointwise boundary equations.',refs=['fichera1967unilateral'],reconstruction=r'The displacement must be defined throughout \(\overline A\), not merely in \(C^1(\Sigma)\), and the prescribed surface work is integrated over \(\Sigma^*\).',development='annotated')
item(176,'P000285-P000286','theorem','Weak existence and rigid-motion compatibility',r'The classical admissible class may contain no minimizer; the discussion passes to weak minimization.',r'If \(a\) is coercive on \(V\), \(J\) has a unique minimizer in every nonempty closed convex \(K\). In pure-traction elasticity \(a\) vanishes on rigid displacements, so one needs a quotient or normalization and compatibility of the work functional with admissible rigid directions; strict negativity in unbounded admissible directions is a typical coercivity condition. Positivity of the strain energy alone is insufficient.',r'\citet[pp.~66--67]{fichera1967unilateral} explicitly treats the necessary work conditions and uniqueness modulo admissible rigid motions. This is an expository report of Fichera\textquotesingle s earlier solution.',refs=['fichera1967unilateral'],reconstruction='The coercive version is stated precisely as a modern sufficient theorem; the notebook omits enough geometric and load data to state Fichera\textquotesingle s most general compatibility theorem verbatim.')
item(176,'P000285-P000286','construction','Scalar boundary-obstacle problem',r'A scalar quadratic functional with uniformly positive coefficient matrix is introduced; on \(\Sigma\), \(u\geq0\) and either the value or the conormal derivative vanishes.',r'Let \(a(x)\) be a bounded measurable symmetric uniformly elliptic matrix on a bounded Lipschitz domain \(A\), let \(K=\{v\in H^1(A):\operatorname{Tr}v\geq0\text{ on }\Sigma\}\), and let \(\ell(v)=\int_A fv+\int_{\Sigma^*}\varphi v\) be continuous. Minimize \(\tfrac12\int_A\nabla v\cdot a\nabla v-\ell(v)\) on \(K\), subject to coercivity or constant-direction compatibility. In a smooth realization the outward conormal derivative on \(\Sigma\) is nonnegative and complementary to \(u\).',r'The scalar analogue is already given in \citet[pp.~66--68]{fichera1967unilateral}; later thin-obstacle regularity cannot be used to assert unrestricted classical solvability for the mixed boundary setup here.',refs=['fichera1967unilateral'])
item(176,'P000286','claim','Necessary sign of total load',r'The notes write \(\int_A f+\int_{\Sigma^*}\varphi\leq0\) as a necessary condition.',r'In the scalar energy convention of the preceding item, every positive constant is an admissible recession direction. If \(J\) is bounded below on \(K\), then necessarily \(\ell(1)=\int_A f+\int_{\Sigma^*}\varphi\leq0\); otherwise \(J(u+c)\to-\infty\) as \(c\to+\infty\). This condition alone does not supply all existence or uniqueness hypotheses.',r'The load condition is part of the classical scalar model in \citet[pp.~66--67]{fichera1967unilateral}. Its elementary recession-direction interpretation also resolves the normal-sign convention.',refs=['fichera1967unilateral'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated')
item(176,'P000286','claim','Failure of classical regularity at a contact interface',r'For signed data \(f\leq0\), \(\varphi<0\), the notebook invokes the maximum principle and Green\textquotesingle s formula and says that no \(C^1(\overline A)\) solution exists; a jump of the normal derivative is indicated.',r'The source does not specify a complete domain, decomposition of its boundary, behavior at \(\overline\Sigma\cap\overline{\Sigma^*}\), or an explicit load. Thus the asserted universal nonexistence cannot be reconstructed as a precise theorem. Mixed contact and load interfaces can obstruct classical regularity even when the variational solution exists.',r'Fichera\textquotesingle s pre-lecture note discusses the distinction between weak existence and regularity \citep[pp.~67--68]{fichera1967unilateral}. It is a partial contextual match; it does not justify quantifying the notebook\textquotesingle s incomplete example over all domains and loads.',refs=['fichera1967unilateral'],cls='F',match='partial',literal='not determinable',status='As of 9 November 2026: weak existence and regularity theory are established, but this particular nonexistence example is not determined by the recorded data.',confidence='medium')
correction('P000285','surface-work term','Integral over Sigma','Integral over Sigma* for the prescribed load','The preceding source assigns the prescribed traction to Sigma*, and the scalar energy on the same page uses Sigma*. The scan faithfully has Sigma in the first formula.','both','faithful_error')
correction('P000285','classical admissible displacements','C^1(Sigma)','C^1(overline A), with the trace inequality on Sigma','The energy contains interior strain derivatives. The scan faithfully writes the boundary symbol.','both','faithful_error')
checkpoint()

talk(177,'1969-09-29','D. A. Kazhdan','P000286-P000287','Artin approximation and proof reductions',r'The analytic approximation theorem is restated in terms of convergent and formal power-series rings. The cusp and reduction to irreducible components provide examples and proof preparation. A Soviet-priority remark names Pokornyi, Rybin and Melamed; its scope must be checked independently of the general theorem.')
art2=item(177,'P000286-P000287','theorem','Formal points and finite-jet approximation',r'The rings of convergent and formal series are denoted \(A\subset B\), and formal solutions of analytic equations are approximated by convergent solutions.',r'For the analytic system and formal solution in 1969-175.3, every prescribed finite jet is realized by an exact convergent solution. Equivalently, the analytic solution set is dense in the formal solution set for the maximal-ideal topology; this does not say that every formal solution converges.',r'The theorem is \citet[Thm.~1]{raynaud1969artin}, reporting \citet{artin1968analytic}; both the 1968 original and June 1969 Bourbaki lecture precede this seminar.',refs=['artin1968analytic','raynaud1969artin'],problem='GP-1969-03',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(177,'P000286','construction','Parametrizing the cusp',r'The example is \(x_1^2-x_2^3=0\), with \(x_1=y^3\), \(x_2=y^2\).',r'The analytic map \(y\mapsto(y^3,y^2)\) satisfies the cusp equation identically and gives its normalization near the origin. The coordinate-ring map \(\mathbb C\{x_1,x_2\}/(x_1^2-x_2^3)\to\mathbb C\{y\}\) sends \(x_1\) to \(y^3\) and \(x_2\) to \(y^2\).',r'This is a classical analytic-curve example illustrating the algebra used in the approximation proof, not a new parametrization claim; compare the local-ring setting of \citet[Section II]{raynaud1969artin}.',refs=['raynaud1969artin'],literal='correct',match='thematic only',reconstruction='The normalization interpretation is explanatory; the notebook records only the parametrization.')
item(177,'P000286-P000287','method','Reduction to irreducible components',r'The argument proposes induction in dimension and writing the defining ideal as an intersection of prime ideals.',r'Let \(R\) be a Noetherian analytic local ring and \(\phi:R\to\mathbb C[[y_1,\ldots,y_s]]\) a local homomorphism. Its kernel is prime and contains a minimal prime of \(R\), so the formal point factors through an irreducible component. Passing first to \(R_{\mathrm{red}}\) removes nilpotents; a radical ideal has a finite intersection decomposition into minimal prime ideals.',r'This is a standard reduction in the proof summarized by \citet[Section II]{raynaud1969artin}. An arbitrary ideal is not itself the intersection of its minimal primes unless it is radical.',refs=['raynaud1969artin'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The domain property of the target and the passage to the reduced analytic germ are supplied explicitly.')
item(177,'P000287','remark','Earlier Soviet work on formal implicit functions',r'The notes cite \ru{Покорный и Рыбкин}, Uspekhi 15(4), 1960, p.~169, and a work of Melamed in the Siberian Mathematical Journal, suggesting an earlier result.',r'The identifiable citation is V.~V. Pokornyi and P.~P. Rybin, \textquotedblleft Stabilization of a process for determining formal implicit functions\textquotedblright{}, pp.~169--172. Its starting point is a scalar formal implicit equation and a Newton-series coefficient procedure. The notebook does not state the exact overlap claimed with multivariable analytic approximation.',r'The full 1960 note was opened and its scalar scope checked \citep{pokornyirybin1960formal}; it was received in December 1958. This verifies the citation and an antecedent, not priority for Artin\textquotesingle s full theorem. Searches of the Melamed/Siberian-Journal lead did not identify a precise matching article.',refs=['pokornyirybin1960formal','artin1968analytic'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='partial',verification='read the result',status='As of 9 November 2026: Pokornyi--Rybin is identified; the claimed equivalence of scope and the Melamed reference remain unresolved.',confidence='medium')
correction('P000287','Soviet-priority author','Rybkin / Рыбкин','Rybin / Рыбин (bibliographic identification)','Math-Net rm6746 identifies P. P. Rybin at exactly UMN 15(4), 1960, pp.169--172. This corrects the identification, not a certainty about the handwritten letters.','both','provenance')
