talk(178,'1969-09-29','L. D. Faddeev','P000287-P000289','Quantization, prequantum operators and the rotation sphere',r'The lecture connects unitary representations to Hamiltonian mechanics, beginning with symplectic forms and the Van Hove operator construction. A sphere orbit and stereographic coordinates illustrate the reduction to holomorphic functions. Several normalizations and global integrality assumptions are absent; the proposed weight must not be mistaken for a proof that a measure exists only at integer parameters. The announced next report on Schmid is a plan, not an additional theorem in this record.')
item(178,'P000287','definition','Symplectic phase space and Liouville measure',r'The notes take a \(2n\)-dimensional phase space with nondegenerate \(\Omega=d\omega\) and invariant measure \(\Omega^n\).',r'A symplectic manifold is a smooth real \(2n\)-manifold with a closed nondegenerate real two-form \(\Omega\); its Liouville volume is \(\Omega^n/n!\). Exactness \(\Omega=d\omega\) is an additional hypothesis or a local choice. In particular a compact symplectic sphere has no globally defined primitive.',r'This is classical symplectic structure underlying the orbit method \citep{kirillov1962unitary}; the line-bundle treatment in \citet[Sections 2 and 4]{kostant1970quantization} makes the global replacement for a primitive explicit.',refs=['kirillov1962unitary','kostant1970quantization'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The exact/local distinction is necessary because the lecture subsequently takes a sphere as its example.')
item(178,'P000287-P000288','theorem','Hamiltonian vector fields and the Poisson bracket',r'The correspondence \(f\mapsto X_f\) preserves brackets, and these vector fields are described as antisymmetric operators in \(L^2(M)\).',r'Fix \(\iota_{X_f}\Omega=-df\) and \(\{f,g\}=X_f g\). Then \([X_f,X_g]=X_{\{f,g\}}\), and each Hamiltonian flow preserves Liouville volume. Consequently \(X_f\) is formally skew-symmetric on compactly supported smooth functions. A globally defined unitary flow additionally requires a complete Hamiltonian vector field; operator self-adjointness requires a domain statement.',r'\citet[Proposition 4.1.1 and Section 4.3]{kostant1970quantization} records the Poisson-algebra homomorphism and explains why global integration is a separate requirement. The construction itself precedes the talk.',refs=['kostant1970quantization'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(178,'P000288','construction','Van Hove prequantization operator',r'The notebook writes \(Z_f=X_f+\alpha(\omega(X_f)-f)\), attributes the construction to Van Hove (1951), and says that the bracket relation remains valid.',r'On an exact symplectic chart with the preceding convention, \(\rho_\alpha(f)=X_f+\alpha(\omega(X_f)-f)\), acting on smooth complex functions, satisfies \([\rho_\alpha(f),\rho_\alpha(g)]=\rho_\alpha(\{f,g\})\). For real \(f\), formal skew-symmetry requires \(\alpha\in i\mathbb R\). Globally, the corresponding operators act on sections of a Hermitian line bundle with compatible connection, when its curvature class is integral.',r'Van Hove\textquotesingle s 1951 paper is verified bibliographically \citep{vanhove1951relations}. The full geometric operator statement is read in \citet[Theorem 4.3.1]{kostant1970quantization}. This is a prequantization construction, not an unrestricted quantization of all observables in an irreducible Hilbert-space model.',refs=['vanhove1951relations','kostant1970quantization'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Signs are fixed by the displayed Hamiltonian convention; scalar multiplication and differentiation are understood on a common smooth domain.')
item(178,'P000288','claim','Dependence on the prequantization parameter',r'The operators for different values of \(\alpha\) are said to give inequivalent representations, and the language also refers to the Lie algebra of vector fields.',r'As representations of the Poisson algebra containing constants, \(\rho_\alpha(1)=-\alpha I\), so distinct values of \(\alpha\) cannot be intertwined by a nonzero linear isomorphism. For \(\alpha\ne0\), this representation does not factor through Hamiltonian vector fields: a constant Hamiltonian has zero vector field but acts nontrivially.',r'The precise distinction is the central extension of Hamiltonian vector fields by constants, described in \citet[Section 4.1]{kostant1970quantization}. The elementary central-character argument explains the notebook\textquotesingle s inequivalence statement.',refs=['kostant1970quantization'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated')
item(178,'P000288','method','Polarization and the free-particle example',r'The notes say that quantum states should depend on half the phase variables, and contrast the classical kinetic energy \(p^2/(2m)\) with the second derivative in the position variable.',r'For \(T^*\mathbb R\), the Schr\"odinger realization uses \(L^2(\mathbb R,dq)\), \(\widehat p=-i\hbar\,d/dq\), and \(\widehat H=-(\hbar^2/2m)d^2/dq^2\), with its usual self-adjoint domain \(H^2(\mathbb R)\). A polarization is the geometric choice selecting an appropriate subspace of sections; an arbitrary choice of half the coordinates is not a coordinate-invariant definition.',r'\citet[Remarks 4.3.1--4.3.2]{kostant1970quantization} separates prequantization and polarized quantization. The free-particle model is standard prior quantum mechanics, not a new theorem of this seminar.',refs=['kostant1970quantization'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',reconstruction='The mass is assumed positive and the real line is boundaryless; other configuration spaces require different self-adjoint boundary conditions.')
item(178,'P000288','construction','Stereographic sphere and holomorphic generators',r'The rotation orbit is the sphere \(x_1^2+x_2^2+x_3^2=a^2\). In stereographic coordinates the notes display \(\Omega=a\,dz\wedge d\bar z/(1+|z|^2)^2\), \(\omega=(a/i)(z\,d\bar z-\bar z\,dz)/(1+|z|^2)\), and a decomposition of a rotation generator into commuting holomorphic and antiholomorphic pieces.',r'The sphere is \(\mathbb{CP}^1\). On a complex chart its real invariant area form is a real multiple of \(i\,dz\wedge d\bar z/(1+|z|^2)^2\); the coefficient is fixed by the total symplectic area. Holomorphic differential operators and their conjugates commute when their coefficients depend only on \(z\) and \(\bar z\), respectively. The two displayed forms in the notebook do not satisfy \(d\omega=\Omega\): direct differentiation gives \(d\omega=(2a/i)\,dz\wedge d\bar z/(1+|z|^2)^2\).',r'The geometric representation mechanism is the rank-one Borel--Weil model \citep[Theorem 4]{serre1954borelweil}. The local coefficients written in the notebook require a normalization correction before they define that model.',refs=['serre1954borelweil','kostant1970quantization'],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='The derivative is an editorial check of the recorded formula, not a recovered replacement coefficient for the original speaker.',development='annotated')
item(178,'P000289','claim','Integrality of the sphere representation',r'The page says that a measure making the operators self-adjoint exists only for integral \(a\), writes a weight \((1+|z|^2)^{-a}\), and obtains a finite space of analytic polynomials.',r'The global condition is that \([\Omega/(2\pi\hbar)]\) be an integral cohomology class. For its nonnegative degree \(k\) on \(\mathbb{CP}^1\), the holomorphic sections of \(\mathcal O(k)\) have dimension \(k+1\) and realize the irreducible \(SU(2)\) representation of highest weight \(k\); it descends to \(SO(3)\) precisely when \(k\) is even. A positive weighted measure \((1+|z|^2)^{-a}d^2z\) itself exists for every real \(a\), and is finite when \(a>1\).',r'Integral line bundles and their irreducible representations are already in \citet[Theorem 4]{serre1954borelweil}; the prequantum integrality criterion is \citet[Proposition 2.1.1]{kostant1970quantization}. Thus integrality belongs to a global equivariant quantization, not to bare existence of a measure.',refs=['serre1954borelweil','kostant1970quantization'],literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction=r'The source parameter \(a\) is not identified with \(k\) until the missing factors of \(i,2,\hbar\) and the area-density convention are fixed. In the stated flat area measure, \(z^j\) is square-integrable exactly when \(a>j+1\); this check prevents an unsupported dimension formula.')
correction('P000288','sphere two-form and primitive',r'Omega=a dz wedge dbarz/(1+|z|^2)^2, omega=(a/i)(z dbarz-barz dz)/(1+|z|^2)',r'd omega=(2a/i) dz wedge dbarz/(1+|z|^2)^2; fix a consistent real symplectic normalization','Direct differentiation of the two formulas visible in the scan; the discrepancy is faithfully transcribed.','both','faithful_error')
correction('P000289','integral parameter statement','A measure exists only for integral a','A global equivariant prequantum line bundle requires an integral curvature class; existence of a positive weighted measure alone does not','The scan confirms the measure wording. The radial integral is finite for every real a>1.','both','faithful_error')
checkpoint()

talk(179,'1969-10-06','Dusa McDuff','P000289-P000290','Factors and uncountably many type II1 examples',r'McDuff introduces von Neumann factors through projection equivalence, their type classification, traces and the hyperfinite example, then states her continuum family of nonisomorphic finite factors. The two relevant Annals papers were published in September 1969, before this October lecture; authorship alone is not a reason to place the result in class A.')
item(179,'P000289','definition','Von Neumann algebras and factors',r'A weakly closed self-adjoint operator algebra \(R\) is considered; it is a factor when its center consists of scalars.',r'A von Neumann algebra on a complex Hilbert space \(H\) is a unital weak-operator closed \(*\)-subalgebra \(M\subset B(H)\). It is a factor if \(Z(M)=M\cap M^{\prime}=\mathbb CI\). Its abstract algebra and its particular representation on \(H\) should be distinguished.',r'This is the classical Murray--von Neumann framework; a checked modern statement is \citet[Chapters 1 and 4]{jones2015vonneumann}.',refs=['jones2015vonneumann'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(179,'P000289','theorem','Matrix factors and type I tensor decompositions',r'For matrix algebras the notes write a tensor decomposition and describe minimal projections.',r'If a factor \(M\subset B(H)\) contains a nonzero minimal projection, there are Hilbert spaces \(K,L\) and a unitary \(H\simeq K\otimes L\) such that \(M=B(K)\otimes I_L\) and \(M^{\prime}=I_K\otimes B(L)\). In finite dimensions this is the ordinary matrix-factor structure. The tensor description does not apply to arbitrary type II or III factors.',r'The type I characterization is classical; see \citet[Theorem 4.2.1]{jones2015vonneumann}.',refs=['jones2015vonneumann'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The minimal-projection hypothesis restricts the tensor factorization to type I.')
item(179,'P000289','theorem','Central decomposition into factors',r'The general algebra is described as a direct integral of factors.',r'For a von Neumann algebra acting on a separable Hilbert space, central decomposition yields a measurable field of factor algebras over a standard measure space, with the original algebra their direct integral, up to the usual almost-everywhere equivalence. The center becomes the diagonal \(L^\infty\) algebra.',r'This is von Neumann\textquotesingle s reduction theory, published in 1949; \citet[Chapter V, Section 1]{connes1994noncommutative} gives the classical theorem and its role. It is a direct integral, not a discrete sum in general.',refs=['connes1994noncommutative'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(179,'P000289-P000290','theorem','Projections generate a von Neumann algebra',r'The factor is said to be generated by its projection operators.',r'For every self-adjoint \(x\in M\), all its spectral projections lie in \(M\), and step-function spectral approximants converge to \(x\). Since every element is a linear combination of two self-adjoint elements, the von Neumann algebra is generated by its projections.',r'This is the spectral-theorem foundation of projection classification, recalled in \citet[Chapters 1 and 4]{jones2015vonneumann}.',refs=['jones2015vonneumann'],literal='correct')
item(179,'P000290','definition','Murray--von Neumann comparison and dimension',r'Projection equivalence, comparison and addition of orthogonal projections are used to define a dimension scale.',r'For projections \(p,q\in M\), write \(p\sim q\) when some partial isometry \(v\in M\) satisfies \(v^*v=p\), \(vv^*=q\), and write \(p\precsim q\) when \(p\) is equivalent to a subprojection of \(q\). In a factor any two projections are comparable. A normal dimension function is invariant under equivalence and additive on orthogonal sums, with normalization dependent on the type.',r'The precise comparison statement is \citet[Definition 6.1.1 and Theorem 6.1.8]{jones2015vonneumann}; it is classical material predating the lecture.',refs=['jones2015vonneumann'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(179,'P000290','theorem','Factor types and projection-dimension ranges',r'The alternatives \(I\), \(II_1\), \(II_\infty\), \(III\) are distinguished by existence of minimal projections and dimension values.',r'For factors on a separable Hilbert space, the standard projection-dimension scales are \(\{0,1,\ldots,n\}\) for \(I_n\), \(\{0,1,2,\ldots,\infty\}\) for \(I_\infty\), \([0,1]\) for normalized \(II_1\), \([0,\infty]\) for \(II_\infty\), and \(\{0,\infty\}\) for \(III\). The type III scale records equivalence of nonzero projections in the countably decomposable factor setting; it is not a finite trace.',r'The classical ranges and the distinction between finiteness of the identity and mere order type are explicitly set out in \citet[Section 6.1, pp.~35--37]{jones2015vonneumann}.',refs=['jones2015vonneumann'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(179,'P000290','claim','Trace, determinant and the limits of matrix analogy',r'The finite factor is said to possess trace, determinant and a minimax principle, with the suggestion that matrix arguments continue.',r'A \(II_1\) factor has a unique faithful normal tracial state \(\tau\), normalized by \(\tau(1)=1\). For invertible \(x\), the Fuglede--Kadison determinant is \(\Delta(x)=\exp\tau(\log|x|)\), a positive real number. Spectral distribution and variational analogues exist, but a general self-adjoint element need not have a discrete list of eigenvalues, and the notebook supplies no particular minimax formula.',r'The determinant is \citet[Section 2]{fugledekadison1952determinant}; the trace is reviewed in \citet[Section 6.1]{jones2015vonneumann}. These verify the identified structures, not the unlimited phrase that all finite-matrix arguments apply.',refs=['fugledekadison1952determinant','jones2015vonneumann'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(179,'P000290','theorem','Uniqueness of the separable hyperfinite II1 factor',r'The approximately finite factor is described as unique.',r'Among \(II_1\) factors with separable predual, the hyperfinite factor is unique up to isomorphism. Here hyperfinite means generated in the weak operator topology by an increasing sequence of finite-dimensional \(*\)-subalgebras. The claim is not uniqueness of all finite factors.',r'The uniqueness is the classical Murray--von Neumann theorem, recalled with proof sketch in \citet[Theorem 15.1.1]{jones2015vonneumann}.',refs=['jones2015vonneumann'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(179,'P000290','remark','Named earlier nonisomorphic factor constructions',r'The notebook lists Schwartz, Ching, Sakai, Dixmier, Lance, Zeller-Meier, Stepin and Golodets in a compressed history of examples.',r'The list by itself contains no specified algebra, invariant or cardinality assertion for each author. It cannot be expanded into separate priority theorems without additional source evidence.',r'McDuff\textquotesingle s author bibliography identifies Ching, Dixmier--Lance, Sakai and Zeller-Meier as antecedents to her 1969 work \citep{mcduff1969uncountably}. That is contextual confirmation; it does not identify every construction in this notebook list.',refs=['mcduff1969uncountably'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='partial',verification='indirect',status='As of 9 November 2026: the historical list is preserved; no unrecorded specific theorem is assigned to every name.',confidence='medium')
item(179,'P000290','theorem','A continuum of pairwise nonisomorphic II1 factors',r'McDuff announces \(\mathfrak c\) nonisomorphic factors of type \(II_1\).',r'There are \(2^{\aleph_0}\) pairwise nonisomorphic type \(II_1\) factors acting on separable Hilbert space.',r'This is exactly the principal result of \citet{mcduff1969uncountably}, Annals 90(2), pp.~372--377. Its author abstract was read and the September 1969 issue date verified; the lecture is 6 October 1969. Hence the chronological class is E, although the speaker is the author. The full proof was not directly read in this task.',refs=['mcduff1969uncountably'],literal='correct',verification='indirect')
checkpoint()

talk(180,'1969-10-13','I. M. Gelfand','P000291-P000292','Current groups, support, and Fock constructions',r'The main questions concern irreducible unitary representations of \(SU(2)\)-valued functions on an interval and the possibility of interval support. Evaluation representations, tensor products, exponential Hilbert space and abelian characters serve as comparisons. The notebook specifies neither regularity of functions nor a group topology; this omission materially changes the questions. Araki\textquotesingle s checked paper uses bounded measurable functions and a one-parameter continuity convention, not an implicit answer in the smooth category.')
current1=item(180,'P000291','question','Classifying irreducible representations of an interval current group',r'For matrices \(u(t)\in SU(2)\), \(0\leq t\leq1\), the notes ask for irreducible unitary representations. No differentiability class or topology is written.',r'After specifying a topological group \(G\) of \(SU(2)\)-valued maps on \([0,1]\), classify its strongly continuous irreducible unitary representations up to unitary equivalence. The choices \(C^0\), \(C^1\), \(C^\infty\), bounded measurable maps, and operator-norm versus strong continuity of the representation define different problems.',r'\citet[Theorem 4.11; group theorem in the arXiv version, Theorem 4.15]{janssensneeb2015norm} classify the norm-continuous irreducible representations in the smooth compact-semisimple setting by finite evaluation tensors. Their preprint circulated 11 February 2013, the paper appeared online in December 2014 and in print in 2015. This is a proper subcategory of all strongly continuous representations. \citet[Chapter 1]{janssensneeb2024positive} impose positive-energy assumptions absent from the notebook; \citet[Definitions 2.1--2.3]{araki1970factorizable} use a different measurable current-group category.',refs=['janssensneeb2015norm','janssensneeb2024positive','araki1970factorizable'],cls='C',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',problem='GP-1969-01',worth='Worth developing only after fixing regularity and continuity, because evaluation classification in the norm-continuous category does not control the strongly continuous category.',status='As of 9 November 2026: strong results exist in precisely restricted categories; the notebook\textquotesingle s unrestricted classification is not a single well-posed problem, and the checked sources do not supply a complete strong-continuity classification.',reconstruction='The topological categories are supplied for comparison, not attributed to the 1969 lecturer.',confidence='high')
current2=item(180,'P000291','question','An irreducible representation with interval support',r'The support is the smallest closed \(F\) such that \(u=1\) on a neighborhood of \(F\) implies \(T_u=I\). The explicit question is whether an irreducible representation can have a whole interval as support.',r'For a chosen function group and continuity convention, ask for an irreducible unitary representation which is nontrivial on the subgroup of functions supported in every nonempty open subinterval. This is a precise full-support formulation of the displayed question. Norm-continuous smooth-current representations have finite support, whereas strongly continuous representations require separate analysis.',r'The finite-support obstruction follows from \citet[Theorem 4.11]{janssensneeb2015norm}. The \(SU(2)\) energy irreducibility theorem of \citet{ismagilov1976unitary} assumes base dimension at least five and cannot answer an interval question; \citet[p.~74]{ismagilov1981segment} proves reducibility of the interval energy representation. The chapter develops an explicit paired-evaluation infinite tensor construction for \(C^1\) maps, also irreducible on smooth maps, and checks full interval support. It does not assert an exact archival topology or novelty.',refs=['janssensneeb2015norm','ismagilov1976unitary','ismagilov1981segment'],cls='C',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',problem='GP-1969-02',worth='Worth developing in a fixed topology, because the contrast between finite norm-continuous support and full strongly continuous support is both explicit and mathematically consequential.',status='As of 9 November 2026: the bounded C^1/smooth strong-continuity existence variant is proved in this chapter; no verdict is asserted for every possible interpretation of the unspecified 1969 group.',development='developed in this chapter',reconstruction='The direct local-subgroup criterion avoids presupposing existence of a smallest closed support for an entirely unspecified class of maps.',confidence='high')
item(180,'P000291','construction','Single-point evaluation representations',r'The simplest example evaluates \(u\) at \(t_0\) and applies an irreducible representation of \(SU(2)\).',r'For \(t_0\in[0,1]\) and the irreducible \(SU(2)\) representation \(V_\ell\) of highest weight \(\ell\geq0\), \(\pi(u)=V_\ell(u(t_0))\) is irreducible because the evaluation homomorphism is onto. It is continuous for each usual \(C^r\) topology. For \(\ell>0\) its support is \(\{t_0\}\); the trivial representation has empty support.',r'This is the elementary evaluation construction, included as the basic family in \citet{janssensneeb2015norm}. It was available before the lecture; the later theorem classifies a specified category, rather than introducing this example.',refs=['janssensneeb2015norm'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(180,'P000291','construction','Finite tensor products at distinct points',r'The notes tensor evaluation representations at finitely many points and describe them as irreducible.',r'At pairwise distinct \(t_1,\ldots,t_m\), the joint evaluation from \(C^r([0,1],SU(2))\) onto \(SU(2)^m\) is surjective for \(r=0,1,\ldots,\infty\). Thus the tensor product of irreducible factor representations is irreducible, with support the finite subset carrying nontrivial factors. Repeated evaluation points must first be combined and decomposed as \(SU(2)\) tensor products.',r'The finite evaluation family is precisely the one in \citet{janssensneeb2015norm}. The interpolation and irreducibility argument is classical and does not depend on the later completeness theorem.',refs=['janssensneeb2015norm'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Distinctness, removal of trivial factors and the regularity convention are made explicit.')
item(180,'P000291','claim','The countably many evaluation points remark',r'The source says that the construction cannot be done for a countable set, followed by a parenthesis that it can be done for a convergent sequence.',r'An infinite tensor product requires choices of reference vectors and convergence conditions for the product unitaries; continuity and irreducibility are additional issues. Cardinality alone is not an obstruction, and the recorded parenthesis provides too little information to recover the intended construction or exclusion theorem.',r'\citet[Section 4]{araki1970factorizable} analyzes discrete and continuous tensor contributions in a specified measurable setting. The chapter\textquotesingle s paired-evaluation construction also demonstrates why a bare prohibition for countable sets is untenable without a category and tensor-sector convention.',refs=['araki1970factorizable'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='partial',status='As of 9 November 2026: the exact intended claim is unidentified; one cannot infer a general nonexistence theorem from this sentence.',confidence='medium')
item(180,'P000291-P000292','construction','Exponential Hilbert space and coherent vectors',r'The notes define \(e^K\) as the sum of symmetric tensor powers and write exponential vectors with normalization \(\sqrt{p!}\).',r'For a complex Hilbert space \(K\), let \(\Gamma_s(K)=\bigoplus_{p=0}^{\infty}\operatorname{Sym}^pK\), where the zeroth summand is \(\mathbb C\). Define \(e(\phi)=\bigoplus_{p\geq0}\phi^{\otimes p}/\sqrt{p!}\). Then \(\langle e(\phi),e(\psi)\rangle=\exp\langle\phi,\psi\rangle\), and these vectors span a dense subspace.',r'This is classical symmetric Fock space, used explicitly in \citet[Section 4]{araki1970factorizable}; his paper was submitted in June 1969 and revised in November, and builds on earlier exponential-space work cited there.',refs=['araki1970factorizable'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The tensor factor repeated in degree p is phi; the handwritten final factor and the distinction between the degree index and vector must not alter the coherent-vector definition.')
item(180,'P000292','theorem','Fock factorization over orthogonal sums',r'The identity \(e^{K_1\oplus K_2}=e^{K_1}\otimes e^{K_2}\) is used to motivate a continuous product.',r'There is a canonical unitary \(\Gamma_s(K_1\oplus K_2)\simeq\Gamma_s(K_1)\otimes\Gamma_s(K_2)\) carrying \(e(\phi_1\oplus\phi_2)\) to \(e(\phi_1)\otimes e(\phi_2)\). It follows by comparing exponential inner products and using their dense spans; direct-integral versions require a specified measurable field and measure.',r'The exponential-space factorization is the framework of \citet[Sections 3--4]{araki1970factorizable}. The displayed finite-sum identity is established independently of any irreducibility claim for a current group.',refs=['araki1970factorizable'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(180,'P000292','construction','A reducible direct-integral Fock representation',r'The notes pass to \(K=\int^\oplus K(t)\,dt\) and explicitly observe that the representation constructed in Fock space is reducible.',r'If \(\rho(u)\) is the decomposable unitary action on a nonzero direct-integral one-particle space, its second quantization \(\Gamma_s(\rho(u))=\bigoplus_{p\geq0}\operatorname{Sym}^p\rho(u)\) preserves each particle-number summand. In particular the vacuum line is invariant, so this representation is reducible whenever a nonzero positive-degree summand is present.',r'Araki\textquotesingle s more general formulas include cocycle displacement terms \citep[Section 4, Lemma 4.2]{araki1970factorizable}; they must not be identified with the simpler number-preserving construction. The notebook\textquotesingle s stated reducibility is correct for the latter.',refs=['araki1970factorizable'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',reconstruction='The exact action is not written fully, so the number-preserving interpretation is stated as the version verified by the recorded direct-integral example.')
item(180,'P000292','construction','Abelian characters from linear functionals',r'For \(\alpha(t)=\exp x(t)\), a character is written as an exponential of a linear functional or generalized function; delta mass gives point evaluation.',r'On the additive real space \(C^\infty([0,1],\mathbb R)\), every continuous real linear functional \(T\) gives the unitary character \(f\mapsto\exp(iT(f))\). It descends along \(f\mapsto e^{if}\) to the smooth \(U(1)\) current group on the interval exactly when \(T(1)\in\mathbb Z\), because two lifts differ by a constant multiple of \(2\pi\). Point evaluation corresponds to a delta functional. For the uniform \(C^0\) topology, continuous functionals are finite signed measures, rather than arbitrary distributions.',r'The abelian factorizable setting is discussed in \citet[Sections 1--2 and 9]{araki1970factorizable}. The interval lifting condition and the topology-dependent dual clarify the notebook\textquotesingle s schematic exponential formula; no classification of nonabelian irreducibles follows from it.',refs=['araki1970factorizable'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction='The factor i, real-valuedness and integral-period condition are supplied to make the formula a unitary group character; the notebook uses informal complex exponential notation.')
